{
  "subtype": "faceted_decomposition",
  "concept": "hardware_hiccups",
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "hardware_issue_type",
      "is_constitutive_facet": false,
      "role": "restatement",
      "rationale": "This column enumerates categories/kinds of hardware issues (Printer, Monitor, Keyboard, etc.). These are TYPES/instances of the concept, not constitutive aspects OF a hardware hiccup. A faceted decomposition would carve a single hardware issue into aspects like severity, affected component, failure mode, user impact, and remediation. Instead this is a single categorical typology of the whole concept, i.e., a restatement of 'what kind of hardware issue it is' rather than a part-of aspect."
    },
    {
      "name": "is_hardware_incident",
      "is_constitutive_facet": false,
      "role": "restatement",
      "rationale": "A Yes/No flag for whether the incident is a hardware incident restates the identity/membership of the whole concept rather than decomposing it into constitutive aspects. It is a filter/scope indicator, not an aspect OF the hardware hiccup."
    }
  ],
  "facet_columns": [],
  "non_facet_columns": ["hardware_issue_type", "is_hardware_incident"],
  "mutual_exclusivity": 0.4,
  "exhaustiveness": 0.05,
  "rationale": "Neither added column is a constitutive facet (meronym) of 'hardware_hiccups'. 'is_hardware_incident' merely flags membership in the concept, and 'hardware_issue_type' provides a single categorical typology of the whole thing. Neither carves the concept into non-overlapping aspects OF a hardware issue (e.g., affected component, failure mode, severity, user impact, resolution). They also functionally overlap: the NULLs in hardware_issue_type (418) exactly mirror the 'No' count in is_hardware_incident (418), showing the type column is essentially a refinement of the same membership signal rather than an orthogonal facet. Because the columns are restatements/scoping flags rather than parts, the facet set is empty, exhaustiveness is near zero, and mutual exclusivity is undermined by the redundancy between the two columns."
}

